Q: How to get friendly device name from DEV_BROADCAST_DEVICEINTERFACE and Device Instance ID A: <p>The struct issue is already solved in your edit — <code>CharSet.Auto</code> + <code>ByValTStr</code> is correct.</p>
<p>For the friendly name: don't parse the interface path manually, and don't go digging in the registry. Both approaches are fragile and unnecessary — <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-hardware/drivers/install/enumerating-installed-device-interface-classes" rel="nofollow noreferrer">everything you need is available through the Configuration Manager API</a>.</p>
<p>The path in <code>dbcc_name</code> (e.g. <code>\\?\USB#VID_05AC&amp;...</code>) is a device interface path. From it you can get the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-hardware/drivers/install/instance-ids" rel="nofollow noreferrer">instance ID</a>, then from the instance ID you can query <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-hardware/drivers/install/devpkey-name--device-instance-" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DEVPKEY_NAME</a> — which is exactly the friendly name shown in Device Manager.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>// NativeMethods.txt (CsWin32):
// CM_Get_Device_Interface_PropertyW
// CM_Locate_DevNodeW
// CM_Get_DevNode_PropertyW
// DEVPKEY_Device_InstanceId
// DEVPKEY_NAME

static unsafe string? GetFriendlyName(string deviceInterface)
{
    uint size = 512 * sizeof(char);
    Span&lt;byte&gt; buf = stackalloc byte[(int)size];

    fixed (byte* pBuf = buf)
    {
        // interface path → instance ID (e.g. &quot;USB\VID_05AC&amp;PID_1294\...&quot;)
        CONFIGRET cr = PInvoke.CM_Get_Device_Interface_PropertyW(
            deviceInterface,
            PInvoke.DEVPKEY_Device_InstanceId,
            out _, pBuf, ref size, 0);

        if (cr != CONFIGRET.CR_SUCCESS) return null;

        string instanceId = new string((char*)pBuf);

        // instance ID → device node handle
        cr = PInvoke.CM_Locate_DevNodeW(
            out uint devinst, instanceId,
            PInvoke.CM_LOCATE_DEVNODE_NORMAL);

        if (cr != CONFIGRET.CR_SUCCESS) return null;

        // device node → DEVPKEY_NAME (&quot;Apple iPhone&quot;, etc.)
        size = 256 * sizeof(char);
        Span&lt;byte&gt; nameBuf = stackalloc byte[(int)size];

        fixed (byte* pName = nameBuf)
        {
            cr = PInvoke.CM_Get_DevNode_PropertyW(
                devinst, PInvoke.DEVPKEY_NAME,
                out _, pName, ref size, 0);

            if (cr != CONFIGRET.CR_SUCCESS) return null;

            string name = new string((char*)pName);
            return name.Length &gt; 0 ? name : null;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usage in your <code>WndProc</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>protected override unsafe void WndProc(ref Message m)
{
    const int WM_DEVICECHANGE   = 0x0219;
    const int DBT_DEVICEARRIVAL = 0x8000;

    if (m.Msg == WM_DEVICECHANGE &amp;&amp; (int)m.WParam == DBT_DEVICEARRIVAL)
    {
        var hdr = (DEV_BROADCAST_DEVICEINTERFACE_W*)m.LParam;
        if (hdr-&gt;dbcc_devicetype == PInvoke.DBT_DEVTYP_DEVICEINTERFACE)
        {
            string? name = GetFriendlyName(new string(hdr-&gt;dbcc_name));
            Console.WriteLine(name ?? &quot;unknown device&quot;);
        }
    }
    base.WndProc(ref m);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Why not the registry or path parsing:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Parsing the interface path (<code>USB#VID_...</code>) to extract a name is not documented behaviour — the format can vary by bus/driver and there is no guaranteed human-readable component in it.</li>
<li>Reading <code>HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Enum\...</code> directly is also unsupported; that subtree is owned by the kernel and its layout is not a stable API.</li>
<li><code>CM_Get_DevNode_PropertyW</code> with <code>DEVPKEY_NAME</code> is the documented, stable way to get what Device Manager shows. It works across USB, HID, Bluetooth, and everything else without any bus-specific logic.</li>
</ul>
<p>The P/Invoke declarations for all three <code>CM_*</code> functions, <code>DEVPROPKEY</code>, <code>CONFIGRET</code>, and the two <code>DEVPKEY_*</code> constants can be auto-generated by <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/CsWin32" rel="nofollow noreferrer">CsWin32</a> — just add the names to <code>NativeMethods.txt</code> and the source generator handles the rest.</p>
<p><strong>Alternative: SetupAPI</strong></p>
<p>It is also possible to get the friendly name via <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-hardware/drivers/install/setupapi" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SetupAPI</a> — <code>SetupDiGetDeviceRegistryProperty</code> with <code>SPDRP_FRIENDLYNAME</code> (falling back to <code>SPDRP_DEVICEDESC</code>). This works, but SetupAPI is partially deprecated: Microsoft's own documentation marks the <code>SPDRP_*</code> property approach as legacy and recommends the unified device property model (<code>DEVPKEY_*</code>) instead. The Configuration Manager path shown above uses that model directly, so prefer it for new code. SetupAPI is still usable if you're maintaining older code or targeting XP-era compatibility.</p>

Q: StreamSubscription.cancel vs StreamController.close on Memory Leaks A: <blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Does calling StreamController.close automatically cancel a StreamSubscription and removes all references?</p>
</blockquote>
</blockquote>
<p>The <code>StreamController.close()</code> method does not cancel a stream subscription.<br />
The <code>StreamController.close()</code> method closes a stream and subscriptions.</p>
<p>When a stream controller closed, it completely removes the reference to the stream subscription.</p>
<p>When your application has no more references to the stream subscription, all memory will be freed.</p>
<p>I haven't checked, but I believe the controller can't cancel a subscription. It can only close the stream, which will close the subscription.<br />
Only the subscriber can cancel the subscription.</p>
<p>Thus, closing a stream and unsubscribing from a stream (canceling the subscription) are not the same thing.</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>import 'dart:async';

import 'package:multitasking/multitasking.dart';

void main() async {
  final streamController = StreamController&lt;int&gt;.broadcast();
  final streamWrapper1 =
      streamController.stream.withSubscriptionTracking(print);
  final sub = streamWrapper1.listen((event) =&gt; print('Value: $event'));
  streamController.add(1);
  streamController.add(2);
  streamController.add(3);
  await Future&lt;void&gt;.delayed(const Duration());
  print('await sub.cancel();');
  await sub.cancel();

  print('-' * 40);

  final streamWrapper2 =
      streamController.stream.withSubscriptionTracking(print);
  streamWrapper2.listen((event) =&gt; print('Value: $event'));
  streamController.add(1);
  streamController.add(2);
  streamController.add(3);
  await Future&lt;void&gt;.delayed(const Duration());
  print('await streamController.close();');
  await streamController.close();
}

</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>SubscriptionEvent.start
Value: 1
Value: 2
Value: 3
await sub.cancel();
SubscriptionEvent.cancel
----------------------------------------
SubscriptionEvent.start
Value: 1
Value: 2
Value: 3
await streamController.close();
SubscriptionEvent.done
</code></pre>

Q: EF Core Table Per Hierarchy: Query with base type in child element &quot;Translation of member &#39;Parent&#39; on entity type &#39;Base&#39; failed&quot; A: <p>I don't believe this will work:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class Parent
{
    public int ID { get; set; }
    public List&lt;D1&gt; D1s { get; set; }
    public List&lt;D2&gt; D2s { get; set; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>That will require 2 FK, one on D1 and one on D2 since the ParentID FK resides on Base, EF won't be able to resolve this down to either a D1 or D2 for the applicable collection.</p>
<p>Think of the mapping such as from the parent perspective:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>.HasMany(p =&gt; p.D1s)
   .WithOne(d1 =&gt; d1.Parent) // Base.ParentId
.HasMany(p =&gt; p.D2s)
   .WithOne(d2 =&gt; d2.Parent) // Base.ParentId  Cannot have 2 mappings to same FK
</code></pre>
<p>EF won't allow two relationships to the same FK. Instead, we can have a single relationship mapped via the base type collection:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class Parent
{
    public int ID { get; set; }
    public List&lt;Base&gt; Children { get; private set; } = [];
    [NotMapped]
    public IEnumerable&lt;D1&gt; D1s =&gt; Children.OfType&lt;D1&gt;();
    [NotMapped]
    public IEnumerable&lt;D2&gt; D2s =&gt; Children.OfType&lt;D2&gt;();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Where The relationship between parent and Base(s) is mapped, and the Bases (Children) need to be eager loaded to access D1s or D2s. Similarly, when querying parents by a D1 or D2, it needs to be done through <code>parent =&gt; parent.Children</code>  To have the different collection references available you would be better off using TPC inheritance mapping.</p>
